\subsection{Extended Training Analysis}
\label{sec:isocompute}

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

We present a KL distillation framework for training contextual sparsity predictors in SwiGLU-based language models (Figure~\ref{fig:overview}).
The method replaces per-neuron binary classification (BCE) with an output-distribution objective that couples all pruning decisions through the model's forward pass and achieves competitive performance without auxiliary error compensation networks at inference.

\subsection{Problem Formulation}
\label{sec:method:formulation}

Consider a pretrained transformer with $L$ layers, each containing a SwiGLU feed-forward network~\cite{shazeer2020glu}.
For layer $\ell$, the FFN computes:
\begin{align}
\mathbf{y}^{(\ell)} = \bigl(\sigma(\mathbf{x}^{(\ell)} \mathbf{W}_g^{(\ell)}) \odot \mathbf{x}^{(\ell)} \mathbf{W}_u^{(\ell)}\bigr) \mathbf{W}_d^{(\ell)},
\label{eq:swiglu}
\end{align}
where $\mathbf{x}^{(\ell)} \in \mathbb{R}^{d}$ is the hidden state after attention ($d{=}4096$), $\sigma$ denotes SiLU, $\mathbf{W}_g^{(\ell)}, \mathbf{W}_u^{(\ell)} \in \mathbb{R}^{d \times d_{\text{ff}}}$ and $\mathbf{W}_d^{(\ell)} \in \mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{ff}} \times d}$ are the gate, up-projection, and down-projection matrices, and $d_{\text{ff}}{=}14{,}336$.

Define the gate activation $\mathbf{g}^{(\ell)} = \sigma(\mathbf{x}^{(\ell)} \mathbf{W}_g^{(\ell)}) \in \mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{ff}}}$.
Each element $g_j^{(\ell)}$ determines the contribution of neuron $j$ to the output.
We introduce a binary mask $\mathbf{m}^{(\ell)} \in \{0,1\}^{d_{\text{ff}}}$ that zeros out pruned neurons:
\begin{align}
\mathbf{y}_{\text{sparse}}^{(\ell)} = \bigl(\mathbf{m}^{(\ell)} \odot \mathbf{g}^{(\ell)} \odot \mathbf{x}^{(\ell)} \mathbf{W}_u^{(\ell)}\bigr) \mathbf{W}_d^{(\ell)}.
\label{eq:sparse_ffn}
\end{align}
Our goal is to learn a mask predictor $f_\theta^{(\ell)}(\mathbf{x}^{(\ell)}) \to \mathbf{s}^{(\ell)} \in (0,1)^{d_{\text{ff}}}$ that produces per-neuron importance scores from $\mathbf{x}^{(\ell)}$.
At inference, the scores are fed to a global top-$k$ allocation mechanism~\cite{liu2025teal} that distributes the sparsity budget across layers non-uniformly, retaining the $(1{-}\rho) \times L \times d_{\text{ff}}$ highest-scoring neurons model-wide for a target sparsity ratio $\rho$.


\subsection{Predictor Architecture}
\label{sec:method:predictor}

Each layer $\ell$ has an independent two-layer MLP predictor:
\begin{align}
f_\theta^{(\ell)}(\mathbf{x}^{(\ell)}) = \text{GELU}(\mathbf{x}^{(\ell)} \mathbf{W}_1^{(\ell)}) \, \mathbf{W}_2^{(\ell)},
\label{eq:predictor}
\end{align}
where $\mathbf{W}_1^{(\ell)} \in \mathbb{R}^{d \times d_b}$ and $\mathbf{W}_2^{(\ell)} \in \mathbb{R}^{d_b \times d_{\text{ff}}}$ with bottleneck dimension $d_b{=}128$.
The predictor takes $\mathbf{x}^{(\ell)}$ (the hidden state after attention, before the FFN) and outputs $d_{\text{ff}}$-dimensional logits $\mathbf{s}^{(\ell)}$, from which the mask is derived.

Each predictor has $(4096 \times 128) + (128 \times 14336) = 2.37\text{M}$ parameters.
With 32 independent predictors (one per layer), the total overhead is 75.9M parameters, less than 1\% of the 8B base model.
The predictors operate independently across layers, requiring no cross-layer communication at inference.


\subsection{Gumbel-Sigmoid Relaxation}
\label{sec:method:gumbel}

The hard mask $m_j = \mathbf{1}[s_j > \text{threshold}]$ is non-differentiable, preventing gradient flow from the KL objective through the mask to the predictor parameters.
We apply Gumbel-Sigmoid relaxation~\cite{maddison2017concrete,louizos2018l0,jang2017gumbel} to produce differentiable soft masks:
\begin{align}
\tilde{m}_j = \sigma_\tau\!\left(\log \frac{s_j}{1 - s_j} + g_1 - g_2\right), \quad g_1, g_2 \sim \text{Gumbel}(0,1),
\label{eq:gumbel}
\end{align}
where $\sigma_\tau(x) = \text{sigmoid}(x / \tau)$ and $\tau$ is a temperature parameter.
The Gumbel noise provides stochastic exploration of mask configurations during training, while the temperature controls sharpness: as $\tau \to 0$, $\tilde{m}_j$ approaches a binary decision.

We anneal $\tau$ linearly from 1.0 to 0.1 over training.
At high temperature, the soft mask permits gradient flow through all neurons for broad exploration.
As temperature decreases, the mask concentrates on confident decisions, approximating the hard allocation used at inference.

\paragraph{Comparison with Straight-Through Estimation.}
The straight-through estimator (STE)~\cite{bengio2013ste} applies a hard threshold in the forward pass and passes gradients through unchanged ($\partial m / \partial s = 1$).
In our setting, STE produces discontinuous gradient signals: small parameter changes can flip many neurons simultaneously. The resulting oscillations in the penalty weight prevent convergence to the target sparsity.
Gumbel relaxation eliminates this pathology and contributes a 33.8\% improvement (PPL 15.77 $\to$ 11.79 at 50\% sparsity), as we verify empirically in \S\ref{sec:experiments}.


\subsection{KL Divergence Training Objective}
\label{sec:method:kl}

Let $\mathbf{z}_{\text{dense}}$ denote the output logits from a full dense forward pass, and $\mathbf{z}_{\text{sparse}}$ the logits from a forward pass with predictor-generated soft masks $\{\tilde{\mathbf{m}}^{(\ell)}\}_\ell$ applied at every layer.
We minimize the forward KL divergence between the dense and sparse output distributions:
\begin{align}
\mathcal{L}_{\text{KL}} = D_{\text{KL}}(p_{\text{dense}} \,\|\, p_{\text{sparse}}) = \sum_{v} p_{\text{dense}}^{(v)} \log \frac{p_{\text{dense}}^{(v)}}{p_{\text{sparse}}^{(v)}},
\label{eq:kl_loss}
\end{align}
where $p_{\text{dense}} = \text{softmax}(\mathbf{z}_{\text{dense}})$ and $p_{\text{sparse}} = \text{softmax}(\mathbf{z}_{\text{sparse}})$, summed over the vocabulary $v$ and averaged over sequence positions.
The base model is frozen; only the predictor parameters receive gradients.

We use forward KL (mass-covering) rather than reverse KL, as the goal is to preserve the dense model's primary predictions rather than explore alternative modes; \S\ref{sec:experiments} validates this choice with ablations over divergence direction (forward KL outperforms JSD by 6.4\% and reverse KL by 8.6\%).
No temperature scaling ($T{=}1$) is applied~\cite{hinton2015distilling}.


\subsection{Adaptive Penalty Controller}
\label{sec:method:lagrangian}

We enforce a model-level sparsity target $\rho$ via constrained optimization with $\bar{s} = \frac{1}{L} \sum_\ell \frac{1}{d_{\text{ff}}} \sum_j \tilde{m}_j^{(\ell)}$ denoting the average retained fraction:
\begin{align}
\min_\theta \; \mathcal{L}_{\text{KL}} \quad \text{s.t.} \quad \bar{s} = 1 - \rho.
\label{eq:constrained}
\end{align}
We relax this via an adaptive penalty controller:
\begin{align}
\mathcal{L} = \mathcal{L}_{\text{KL}} + \lambda \cdot \text{clamp}(|\bar{s} - (1 - \rho)| - \epsilon, \min{=}0)^2,
\label{eq:lagrangian}
\end{align}
where $\epsilon = 0.02$ is a deadzone margin. The penalty weight $\lambda$ is updated via geometric doubling (doubled when sparsity exceeds target, halved otherwise), clamped to $[\lambda_{\text{floor}}, \lambda_{\max}]$, reaching target sparsity within 40--110 steps across all tested regimes (Appendix~\ref{app:lagrangian}, Figure~\ref{fig:lagrangian_dynamics}).


\subsection{Gradient Analysis: BCE vs.\ KL}
\label{sec:method:gradient}

To build intuition for how KL distillation discovers neuron importance patterns that diverge from magnitude-based masks (\S\ref{sec:mask_analysis}), we contrast the gradient structure of the two objectives. This analysis is suggestive; \S\ref{sec:mask_analysis} provides evidence that the coupled gradient discovers mask targets that yield lower perplexity, with mask quality driving inference-stage quality.

Under BCE, the predictor trains against oracle masks $m_j^* = \mathbf{1}[|g_j| \geq \text{top-}k\text{ threshold}]$ derived from activation magnitudes.
The gradient $\partial \mathcal{L}_{\text{BCE}} / \partial s_j = \sigma(s_j) - m_j^*$ depends only on neuron $j$; each neuron is trained independently with no inter-neuron coupling.
Under KL distillation, the chain rule introduces a downstream Jacobian $\partial \mathbf{z}_{\text{sparse}} / \partial \tilde{m}_j^{(\ell)}$ that couples neuron $j$'s gradient to all other retained neurons in layers $\ell$ through $L$, because the sparse hidden state propagates through subsequent attention and FFN operations.
This coupling allows KL to evaluate each neuron's importance in the context of the full sparse computation, rather than against a fixed magnitude target.

Gradient SNR measurements\footnote{$\text{SNR}_\ell = \|\mathbb{E}[\nabla_{\theta_\ell} \mathcal{L}]\|_2^2 / \text{Var}[\nabla_{\theta_\ell} \mathcal{L}]$, expectation over mini-batches.} confirm a structural difference: at step 300 (of 1000), KL achieves $5.14\times$ higher SNR than BCE ($5.877{\times}10^{-3}$ vs.\ $1.144{\times}10^{-3}$, LLaMA-3.1-8B, C4; Appendix~\ref{app:snr_extended}).
We note that the SNR trajectory is non-monotonic: BCE briefly surpasses KL during steps 100--250 before KL establishes sustained advantage from step 300 onward (Appendix~\ref{app:snr_extended}).
The coupled gradient provides a mechanism by which KL can discover mask patterns that diverge from magnitude ranking; the target-swap ablation in \S\ref{sec:mask_analysis} confirms that the discovered mask targets, not the coupling mechanism itself, drive inference-stage quality.

